Non-fullerene polymer solar cells (PSCs) attract more attention due to the constantly refreshed power conversion efficiency (PCE) based on the versatile non-fullerene acceptors. In this work, the PCEs of $10.51\%$ and $11.02\%$ are obtained for the two kinds of non-fullerene PSCs with IDT6CN-M or ITCPTC as acceptor and PBDB-T as donor, respectively. ITCPTC has relatively narrow bandgap and high absorption coefficient compared with IDT6CN-M, which well explains the relatively large short-circuit current density of $17.44~\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ for ITCPTC based binary PSCs. Meanwhile, IDT6CN-M based binary PSCs exhibit the relatively high fill factor (FF) of $75.3\%$ and open-circuit voltage of $_{0.915\mathrm{~V~}}$ . The PCE of $11.92\%$ and FF of $76.5\%$ are achieved for the ternary PSCs with $60~\mathsf{w t\%}$ ITCPTC content in acceptors, which should be attributed to the enhanced photon harvesting and their good compatibility for synergistic improvement of exciton utilization and charge transport in the ternary active layers. The FF of $76.5\%$ should be the top value of ternary non-fullerene PSCs.
## Introduction
Ternary strategy has been commonly demonstrated as an efficient method to improve the performance of polymer solar cells (PSCs), especially in the past five years.1-3 The preliminary criteria of the third component selection is the complementary photon harvesting range with host donor:acceptor (D:A) blend system, which is inspired from tandem solar cells. In fact, the third component is mixed into host blend solution to prepare bulk heterojunction active layer, which makes the dynamic process more complex in ternary active layers. The more characteristics of the third component should be considered for simultaneously optimizing photon harvesting, exciton dissociation and utilization, charge transport and collection in ternary active layers.4,5 Recently, efficient non-fullerene acceptors have been well developed to markedly improve the power conversion efficiency (PCE) of PSCs, which also provide more selections to prepare efficient ternary PSCs.6-11 Here, recent development of efficient ternary PSCs with two acceptors (one fullerene/one non-fullerene or two nonfullerenes) is summarized in Table 1. Ternary PSCs with PCE over $9\%$ were elaborately selected to give the more objective statistical results. More than $10\%$ PCE improvement can be obtained by employing ternary strategy compared with the corresponding binary PSCs, also keeping the simple fabrication technology. In fact, there are only two cases for binary PSCs with PCE over $10\%$ and ternary PSCs with PCE over $12\%$ . There are enough room for PCE improvement of ternary PSCs based on two highly efficient binary PSCs. It is universally recognized that the short-circuit current density $(J_{S C})$ of ternary PSCs can be markedly increased due to the enhanced photon harvesting by the third component. The key issues are how to simultaneously improve the other parameters for obtaining highly efficient ternary PSCs, especially for fill factor (FF) of ternary PSCs. As seen from the Table 1, the improvement on FF should be a feasible strategy in ternary PSCs, although charge traps are unavoidable in ternary active layer due to the different energy levels of used materials.12,13 The more deep investigations should be carried out to clarify its working mechanism. The working mechanism of ternary PSCs have been commonly reported, such as energy transfer (ET), charge transfer (CT), parallel-linkage model (PLM) and alloy model (AM).14-17 The third component was also used as morphology regulator or nucleating agent to adjust molecular arrangement of ternary active layers.18-20 The guideline between used materials and working mechanism has not been built up for guiding selection of the third component and device design. The corresponding binary PSCs should have complementary photovoltaic parameters to optimize the performance of ternary PSCs. Many detailed investigations should be carried out to formulate selection criteria of the third component according to the given host D:A blend system.
Novel donor materials need to be well developed for the better matching with the rapid development of non-fullerene acceptor materials, resulting in the constantly refreshed champion PCE of binary non-fullerene PSCs. 36-39 In this work,  poly[(2,6-(4,8-bis(5-(2- ethylhexyl)thiophen-2-yl)-benzo[1,2-b:4,5-b’]dithiophene))-alt-(5,5- $(1^{\prime},3^{\prime}$ -di-2-thienyl-5’,7’-bis(2-ethylhexyl)benzo[1’,2’-c:4’,5’- c’]dithiophene-4,8-dione))] (PBDB-T) was selected as electron donor.40 The non-fullerene materials (3,9-bis(2-methylene-(3-(1,1- dicyanomethylene)-cyclopentane-1,3-dione-[c]thiophen))- 5,5,11,11-tetrakis(4-hexylphenyl)-dithieno[2,3-d:20,30-d0]-sindaceno[1,2-b:5,6-b0]dithiophene) (ITCPTC) and (4,4,9,9- tetrakis(4-hexylphenyl)-4,9-dihydro-s-indaceno[1,2-b]thiophene-alt[5,6-d]thieno[3,2-b]thiophene)-2-(5/6-methyl-3-oxo-2,3-dihydro1H-inden-1-ylidene)malononitrile (IDT6CN-M) were selected as electron acceptors.41 The IDT6CN-M and ITCPTC can work well with PBDB-T to deliver PCE of $11.02\%$ and $10.51\%$ , respectively. Meanwhile, ITCPTC has a narrower bandgap and slightly higher absorption coefficient compared with IDT6CN-M. The ITCPTC based PSCs exhibit a relatively large $J_{S C}$ of $17.44~\mathsf{m A}/\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ . The IDT6CN-M based PSCs exhibit the relatively large FF of $75.3\%$ and open-circuit voltage $(V_{O C})$ of 0.915 V. According to the characteristics of two acceptors and photovoltaic parameters of binary PSCs, a series of ternary PSCs were prepared with different ITCPTC content in acceptors. The PCE of the optimized ternary PSCs arrives to $11.92\%$ with $60~\mathsf{w t\%}$ ITCPTC content in acceptors, which results from the simultaneously enhanced $J_{S C}$ of $17.81\mathrm{mA/cm}^{2}$ and FF of $76.5\%$ . The improvement of $J_{S C}$ and FF can adequately make up the slightly decreased $V_{O C},$ resulting in more than $8\%$ or $13\%$ PCE improvement compared with the binary PSCs with PCE of $11.02\%$ or $10.51\%$ . The PCE improvement of ternary PSCs can be attributed to the enhanced photon harvesting and their good compatibility for the better exciton utilization and charge transport in the ternary active layers. The experimental details are described in the Supporting Information.
## Results and discussion
Fig. 1a displays the chemical structures of PBDB-T, ITCPTC, IDT6CNM and the schematic diagram of device structure. A series of PSCs were fabricated with an conventional structure of ITO/PEDOT:PSS/active layer/PDIN/Al, the only difference is ITCPTC content in acceptors. The absorption spectra of neat films were measured and the normalized absorption spectra are shown in Fig. 1b. PBDB-T film exhibits wide photon harvesting range with a major absorption peak at $630\ \mathsf{n m}$ . IDT6CN-M and ITCPTC films show strong photon harvesting ability in long wavelength range with absorption peak at $685~\mathsf{n m}$ and $725\ n m$ , respectively. There are obviously complementary absorption spectra of used materials, which may provide great potential to prepare efficient ternary PSCs by optimizing ITCPTC content in acceptors for harvesting more photon as much as possible. The absorption coefficients of IDT6CNM and ITCPTC were measured and are shown in Fig. S1, the maximum molar extinction coefficients of IDT6CN-M and ITCPTC are approximate $2.0{\times}10^{5}\mathsf{M}^{-1}\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ . Fig. 1c shows the absorption spectra of D:A blend films with different ITCPTC content in acceptors. The optimized photon harvesting in the whole wavelength range should be obtained by adjusting ITCPTC content in acceptors, which is the prerequisite for preparing highly efficient PSCs.
The current density-voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ curves of all PSCs with different ITCPTC content in acceptors were measured under AM 1.5G illumination with light intensity of $100\mathrm{\mw/cm}^{2}$ , as shown in Fig. 2a and Fig. S2. A PCE of $11.02\%$ is achieved for the PBDB-T:IDT6CN-M based PSCs, with a $J_{S C}$ of $16.00\mathrm{mA/cm}^{2}$ , a $v_{o c}$ of $_{0.915\vee}$ and a FF of $75.3\%$ . The PBDB-T:ITCPTC based PSCs exhibit a PCE of $10.51\%$ with a $J_{S C}$ of $17.44~\mathrm{mA/cm}^{2}$ , a $v_{o c}$ of $0.836\ensuremath{\vee}$ and a FF of $72.1\%$ . Obviously, the $J_{S C}$ of PBDB-T:ITCPTC based PSCs is larger than that of PBDBT:IDT6CN-M based PSCs, which can be well explained from the corresponding absorption spectra. Meanwhile, the two kinds of binary PSCs exhibit the rather high FFs $(>72\ \%)$ , suggesting that ideal charge transport channels or phase separation should be formed in the two kinds of binary active layers. It is highlighted that the $J_{{S C}}\mathsf{s}$ and FFs of ternary PSCs can be simultaneously improved along with ITCPTC content in acceptors up to $60\mathrm{\u}\%$ and then decreased for incorporating more ITCPTC. The PCE arrives to $11.92\%$ for ternary PSCs with $60\mathrm{\u}\mathrm{t}\% $ ITCPTC in acceptors, resulting from the enhanced $J_{S C}$ of $17.81\mathrm{mA/cm}^{2}$ , FF of $76.5\%$ and an acceptable $V_{O C}$ of $0.875\mathtt{V}.$ It is also worth highlighting that the FF of $76.5\%$ indicates the good compatibility among used materials, which should be the top value of ternary non-fullerene PSCs. The external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra of all PSCs were measured and are shown in Fig. 2b. According to the EQE spectra, the calculated $J_{{S C}}\mathsf{s}$ are slightly less than the measured values with the deviations less than $3\%$ due to the cells without encapsulation for EQE measurement. The measured and calculated key parameters of PSCs are summarized in Table 2. The EQE spectra of ternary PSCs are gradually extended toward long wavelength range along with the increase of ITCPTC content in acceptors, which can be well explained from the absorption spectra of the corresponding blend films. Meanwhile, apparent difference can be observed between the EQE spectra of all PSCs and the absorption spectra of corresponding blend films. Although all blend films have rather weak absorption for light with wavelengths less than $550~\mathsf{n m}$ , the PSCs also exhibit relatively high EQE values. To clarify the underlying reason, the absorption spectra of active layers (in real cells) were calculated according to the reflection spectra of corresponding cells and are shown in Fig. 2c. The reflection spectra of all PSCs and a special device
ITO/PEDOT:PSS/PMMA $(\sim120\ \mathsf{n m}$ )/PDIN/Al were measured and are shown in Fig. S3. It is apparent that the absorption spectra of active layers are different from those of blend films, which is attributed to the interference effect between incident light and reflected light from Al electrode.42,43 The absorption spectral shape of active layers is very similar with the EQE spectral shape of corresponding PSCs.
Although both $J_{{S C}}\mathsf{s}$ and FFs of ternary PSCs are improved along with ITCPTC content up to $60\mathrm{\wt\%}$ , the $V_{O C^{\mathsf{S}}}$ of ternary PSCs exhibit a monotonously decreased trend, as shown in Fig. 2d. For PSCs with the same interfacial layers and electrodes, the $V_{O C}$ strongly depends on the energy levels of used materials.44 It is known that photogenerated holes will be transported along the channels formed by donor PBDB-T. And photogenerated electrons will be transported along the channels formed by IDT6CN-M and ITCPTC in ternary PSCs. The energy levels of electron transport channels will vary along with the change of ITCPTC content in acceptors. The oxidation and reduction potential onsets of acceptor blend films were measured by cyclic voltammetry (CV) method, as shown in Fig. S4. The highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) and the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) energy levels of ITCPTC:IDT6CN-M blend films were calculated according to the CV curves. The key parameters of acceptor blend films are listed in Table S1. The HOMO and LUMO energy levels of ITCPTC:IDT6CN-M blend films dependence on the ITCPTC content are shown in Fig. 2e. Obviously, LUMO energy levels of the acceptor blend films are gradually decreased along with the increase of ITCPTC content, indicating that the alloy states of ITCPTC and IDT6CN-M may be formed in the acceptor blend films.32 The decreased $V_{O C^{\mathsf{S}}}$ of ternary PSCs can be explained from the decreased LUMO levels of acceptor blend films. The alloy model has been commonly proposed to explain the linearly varied $V_{O C^{\mathsf{S}}}$ dependence on the third component content in ternary PSCs.45,46 To further confirm the efficient ternary strategy, the statistical PCE distribution histogram of binary and the optimized ternary PSCs is shown in Fig. 2f. The average PCEs are $10.76\%$ , $10.32\%$ and $11.73\%$ for IDT6CN-M, ITCPTC based binary PSCs and the optimized ternary PSCs, respectively. The average PCEs were calculated according to 20 cells prepared from different batches. The detailed $\jmath_{-}\chi$ curves and photovoltaic parameters of the optimized ternary PSCs are exhibited in Fig. S5 and Table S2. The shunt resistance $(R_{s h})$ and series resistance $(R_{s})$ of all PSCs were calculated according to the $\jmath_{-}\chi$ curves, as listed in Table 2. The $R_{s h}$ is increased and $R_{S}$ is decreased until the ITCPTC content increases to $60~\mathrm{wt\%}$ in acceptors. And then $R_{s h}$ is decreased and $R_{S}$ is increased by incorporating more ITCPTC in acceptors. The optimized ternary PSCs simultaneously exhibit the minimum $R_{s}$ of $3.23\Omega\ c m^{2}$ and the maximum $R_{S h}$ of $1830\Omega\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}.$ , which are beneficial to charge transport and collection for obtaining the relatively high FF of $76.5\%$
The intermolecular interaction between IDT6CN-M and ITCPTC was further investigated to clarify the working mechanism of ternary PSCs. Fig. 3a exhibits absorption spectrum of ITCPTC and photoluminescence (PL) spectrum of IDT6CN-M, the apparent
