spectral overlap is beneficial to energy transfer from IDT6CN-M to ITCPTC.47 To further confirm the energy transfer from IDT6CN-M to ITCPTC, PL spectra of acceptor blend films with different ITCPTC content were measured under $650~\mathsf{n m}$ light excitation and are shown in Fig. 3b. The thicknesses of neat and blend films are about $100\ \mathsf{n m}$ for PL measurement. Both neat ITCPTC and ITD6CN-M films exhibit broad PL spectra with different emission peak at $730\mathsf{n m}$ and $770~\mathsf{n m}$ , resulting in an apparent PL spectral overlap. Meanwhile, the PL intensity of neat IDT6CN-M film is much stronger than that of neat ITCPTC film under the same excitation condition. The PL emission of blend films exhibit a broader spectral range and redshifted emission peak along with the increase of ITCPTC content. The red-shifted PL emission peak of blend films should be mainly attributed to the enhanced PL emission intensity of ITCPTC and the decreased PL emission intensity of IDT6CN-M along with the increase of ITCPTC content in the blend films. The enhanced PL emission intensity of ITCPTC should be attributed to its increased content and the energy transfer from IDT6CN-M to ITCPTC.  It is apparent that PL emission intensity of acceptor blend films with 80 wt% ITCPTC is stronger than that of neat ITCPTC film under the same excitation condition, which can further confirm the efficient energy transfer from IDT6CN-M to ITCPTC. The transient absorption spectra of acceptor blend films were investigated to further confirm the energy transfer process, as exhibited in Fig. S6. It is apparent that ground state bleach (GSB) signal at $750~\mathsf{n m}$ is rather weak for neat IDT6CN-M film, which can be selected as the characteristic signal to monitor the energy transfer in the acceptor blend films. The $750~\mathsf{n m}$ decay curves of neat and blend films abstracted from transient absorption spectra are shown in Fig. 3c. The corresponding fitted lifetimes are listed in Table S3. The lifetime of
$750\mathsf{n m}$ is approximate 1.03 ps for neat ITCPTC film and is gradually increased to 2.21 ps for the blend films with $20\mathrm{\ut\%}$ ITCPTC, which should result from the energy transfer from IDT6CN-M to ITCPTC.48 The utilization of IDT6CN-M exciton can be improved through energy transfer process in ternary PSCs. To exclude charge transfer between two acceptors, a series of cells were prepared with neat IDT6CN-M or ITCPTC or their blend as the active layers. The $J{-}V$ curves of the cells were measured under $\mathsf{A M}1.5\mathsf{G}$ illumination with light intensity of $100\mathrm{\mw/cm}^{2}$ and are shown in Fig. S7. The $J_{S C}$ of cells with IDT6CN-M:ITCPTC as active layers is in between that of cells with IDT6CN-M or ITCPTC as active layers, indicating that exciton dissociation at IDT6CN-M/ITCPTC interface should be negligible.49 According to the above analysis, the dynamic processes in ternary PSCs can be schematically depicted in Fig. 3d. The two acceptors prefer to form an alloy state with mixed energy levels due to their good compatibility. The alloy state of two acceptors may be beneficial to energy transfer from IDT6CN-M to ITCPTC and efficient electron transport in ternary active layers,50 which can be further confirmed from the rather high FF of $76.5\%$ .
