arrangement in the blend films. Fig. 5b and 5c exhibit the out-ofplane (OOP) and in-plane (IP) line-cuts of blend films with different ITCPTC content in acceptors. All blend films exhibit obvious (100) diffraction peaks at $q=0.31\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{-1}$ in OOP and IP directions. To further confirm diffraction peaks information, the OOP line-cuts of neat PBDB-T, ITCPTC and IDT6CN-M were measured, as shown in Fig. S11. The neat PBDB-T film exhibits a relatively strong (100) diffraction peak at $q_{z}=0.31\ \mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{-1}$ . The neat ITCPTC and IDT6CN-M films exhibit relatively weak (100) diffraction peaks at $q_{z}=0.35\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{-1}$ and $0.40\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{-1}$ , respectively. According to the peak location of neat PBDB-T film, the (100) diffraction peaks of blend films reflect molecular orientation and crystallinity of PBDB-T. The acceptors of ITCPTC and IDT6CN-M should be well mixed into PBDB-T matrix. The (100) diffraction peak in OOP direction and (010) diffraction peak in IP direction represent the edge-on orientation, while the (100) diffraction peak in IP direction and (010) diffraction peak in OOP direction represent the face-on orientation.54 For the optimized ternary blend films, the (100) peak intensity was markedly enhanced in IP direction. The rather weak (100) peak intensity is observed in OOP direction. This phenomenon indicates that PBDB-T prefers to form face-on orientation in the optimized ternary blend films, which should be propitious to hole transport along the normal direction of substrates. The optimized PBDB-T molecular arrangement can be also supported by the increased hole mobility and improved FF of the optimized ternary PSCs. To further confirm the positive effect of ternary strategy on the performance of PSCs, the stability of typical PSCs were measured and are shown in Fig. S12. The stability of optimized ternary PSCs is slightly better than that of binary PSCs, which may be due to the optimized morphology and molecular arrangement in the ternary active layers. Meanwhile, the inverted PSCs were fabricated with a structure of ITO/ZnO/Active layer $\mathsf{M o O}_{3}/\mathsf{A g}$ . The $\jmath_{-}\chi$ curves of binary and the optimized ternary inverted PSCs were measured under AM 1.5G illumination with light intensity of $100\mathrm{\mw/cm}^{2}$ , as shown in Fig. S13. The key photovoltaic parameter of inverted PSCs are summarized in Table S4. The PCE of optimized ternary inverted PSCs is about $11.58\%$ , which is better than $10.93\%$ of the inverted PSCs with IDT6CN-M as acceptor and $10.19\%$ of the inverted PSCs with ITCPTC as acceptor. The ternary strategy as an efficient and simple method on PCE improvement can be demonstrated from the inverted PSCs once again.
## Conclusions
In summary, two kinds of binary non-fullerene PSCs were fabricated with one donor PBDB-T and different bandgap non-fullerene acceptor IDT6CN-M or ITCPTC, respectively. A PCE of $11.02\%$ is achieved for the IDT6CN-M based PSCs, with a $J_{S C}$ of $16.00\mathrm{\mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , a $V_{O C}$ of $_{0.915\vee}$ and a FF of $75.3\%$ . The ITCPTC based PSCs exhibit a PCE of $10.51\%$ with a $J_{S C}$ of $17.44\mathrm{mA/cm}^{2},$ , a $v_{o c}$ of $0.836\ensuremath{\vee}$ and a FF of $72.1\%$ . Based on the efficient binary PSCs, a series of ternary PSCs were fabricated with different ITCPTC content in acceptors. The PCE of ternary PSCs arrives to $11.92\%$ with a $J_{S C}$ of 17.81 ${\mathsf{m A}}/{\mathsf{c m}}^{2}.$ , a ${v_{o c}}$ of $0.875\mathrm{~V~}$ and a FF of $76.5\%$ for $60~\mathsf{w t\%}$ ITCPTC content in acceptors. The PCE of $11.92\%$ and FF of $76.5\%$ are among the top values for ternary non-fullerene PSCs. Approximate $8\%$ or $13\%$ PCE improvement was achieved compared with IDT6CN-M or ITCPTC based binary PSCs, respectively. Addition to the complementary absorption of IDT6CN-M and ITCPTC, the advantages of binary PSCs are inherited in the optimized ternary PSCs. The photovoltaic parameters of binary PSCs should be well considered as the selection criterion of materials for obtaining efficient ternary PSCs.
