## Broader context
Recently, ternary polymer solar cells (PSCs) with two acceptors exhibit great potential to achieve high power conversion efficiency (PCE) due to the rapid progress of highly efficient non-fullerene acceptors. Compared to the fullerene derivatives, non-fullerene acceptors process many advantages, such as strong absorption in visible and NIR region, tunable energy levels as well as low cost. In ternary PSCs, the third component plays critical roles on increasing photon harvesting, facilitating exciton dissociation and charge transport. The key photovoltaic parameters of ternary PSCs can be simultaneously improved by elaborately selecting the third component and optimizing device fabrication technology. The fill factor (FF) improvement is still a big challenge owing to the complicated morphology affected by many factors in ternary PSCs. Until now, ternary PSCs with PCE over $11\%$ and FF over $75\%$ are never reported. In this manuscript, the PBDB-T:ITCPTC based PSCs exhibit a PCE of $10.51\%$ with a relatively large short-circuit current density $(J_{S C})$ of $17.44~\mathrm{mA/cm}^{2}$ . The PBDB-T:IDT6CN-M based PSCs exhibit a PCE of $11.02\%$ with a relatively large open-circuit voltage $(V_{O C})$ of 0.915 V and FF of $75.3\%$ . By inheriting the advantages of two binary PSCs, the PCE of $11.92\%$ and FF of $76.5\%$ are achieved for ternary PSCs with $60\mathrm{\textperthousand}$ ITCPTC content in acceptors, which should be among the top values of ternary non-fullerene PSCs.
## Table of Contents
Highly efficient ternary non-fullerene PSCs are fabricated by employing PBDB-T as donor and the mixed ITCPTC:IDT6CN-M as acceptors. The PCE of $11.92\%$ and FF of $76.5\%$ are obtained for ternary PSCs with $60\ \mathsf{w t\%}$ ITCPTC in acceptors, which should be among the top values for ternary non-fullerene PSCs.
